ion Values accuracy is about 96–97%, higher than Cell Sentence at about 93%.\n- Error bars are shown for all bars, indicating variability or uncertainty, but the exact statistic is not specified.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Cells are referenced as the evaluated biological objects, specifically “All Cells” and “Test Cells.”\n- No cell images, tissues, organisms, genes, or microscopy data are directly visible.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The chart compares two input or representation types: “Expression Values” and “Cell Sentence.”\n- No model architecture, workflow, or transformation pipeline is shown in this cropped panel."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 2, "caption": "Figure 2: Cell type annotation results with different cell representation methods. (a) Cell type annotation accuracies on the full dataset and test set. Using cell sentences as cell representation leads to significant accuracy degradation. (b-d) UMAP visualization of classification results and the ground truth. Classification using cell sentences yields a lower accuracy score and exhibits poorer recognition capabilities in certain cell clusters.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a scientific visualization panel labeled “(c)” with the caption “Error cases with Cell Sentence.” It shows a 2D scatter/embedding plot of many small points, likely representing cell-level objects or samples. Points are colored by classification outcome: gray for “Correct” and red for “Incorrect,” according to the legend in the lower right.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Panel label: “(c)”\n- Title/annotation: “Error Rate: 6.42%” in red at the top right\n- Legend: gray = Correct, red = Incorrect\n- Main plot: irregular clustered point cloud/embedding with red incorrect cases distributed throughout several regions\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- The label “Cell Sentence” suggests the plotted objects are cell-derived representations or cell samples, but the image itself does not specify the organism, tissue, assay, or modality.\n\nTransformation/model interface:\n- The figure appears to visualize model prediction errors over a low-dimensional embedding or spatial projection of cell representations.\n- The model/interface named in the visible text is “Cell Sentence.”\n\nFinding shown:\n- The reported error rate is 6.42%.\n- Incorrect predictions are present across the embedding, with some local concentrations, while most points are marked correct."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 2, "caption": "Figure 1: Comparison between the scRNA-seq results and the textual descriptions of a cell.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a small scientific plot labeled “Error cases with Expression Values” with panel marker “(b)”. It shows a 2D embedding/scatter plot, likely of biological observations such as cells or spots, rendered as many gray points.\n\nVisible elements:\n- A legend at lower right with gray points labeled “Correct” and red points labeled “Incorrect”.\n- Red text in the upper right: “Error Rate: 0.76%”.\n- Gray point clusters forming several separated manifold-like regions.\n- Sparse red points overlaid on the gray embedding, marking error cases.\n- No visible axes labels, tick marks, scale bars, or biological object names.\n- No visible model architecture or interface, only model/result classification correctness over an embedding.\n\nFinding shown:\n- The plotted error cases are rare and scattered across the embedding, with an overall reported error rate of 0.76%."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 2, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a scientific figure panel labeled “Ground Truth (d)”. It shows a 2D scatter/embedding plot with many colored points grouped into distinct clusters. A legend on the right lists biological cell-type labels:\n\n- B cells\n- CD4 T cells\n- CD8 T cells\n- CD14+ Monocytes\n- Dendritic Cells\n- Megakary. Monocytes\n- FCGR3A+ Monocytes\n- NK cells\n- Other\n\nVisible finding: the ground-truth annotation separates immune cell populations into colored clusters in a low-dimensional embedding. CD14+ monocytes form a large orange cluster, CD8 T cells appear as a blue upper cluster, NK cells as a red right-side cluster, and other labeled cell types form smaller neighboring clusters. No model interface, preprocessing transform, or method name is visible beyond the ground-truth panel label."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Figure 3: Overview of scMMGPT. (1) Cross-modal Discriminative Objective: Given paired cell and text inputs, the model learns to identify the correct textual description of a cell by aligning the outputs of the scLLM and text LLM. (2, 3) Cross-modal Generative Objectives: scMMGPT strengthens multimodal alignment through a unified generative pre-training strategy, jointly optimizing cell-to-text and text-to-cell translation tasks to facilitate bidirectional knowledge transfer.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic workflow for multimodal cell information modeling using single-cell RNA-seq data and text.\n\nVisible components:\n\n- Left panel: “Multimodal Cell Information”\n  - Shows a human body/lung illustration with arrows labeled “scRNA-seq” and “Biological Databases.”\n  - Shows an “Experimental Evidence in scRNA-seq Matrices” block: a heatmap-like matrix with rows labeled Cell 1, Cell 2, …, Cell N and columns labeled Gene 1, Gene 2, …, Gene M.\n  - Shows “Domain Knowledge in Textual Descriptions”: an example text box describing a “classical monocyte,” sourced from human lung female donor samples.\n\n- Middle panel: “scRNA-seq Data”\n  - Gene expression values are transformed into “Gene Tokens.”\n  - Example gene-expression bar/sequence includes gene names such as SEC23B, MT-CO2, RPL37, MT-CYB and values like 127, 3, 18, 69.\n  - Text descriptions are transformed into a “Text Token Sequence,” with tokens such as “[BOS], This, cell, is, a, …, [EOS].”\n\n- Main model panel:\n  - A blue “scLLM” block processes gene tokens.\n  - An orange “Text LLM” block processes text tokens.\n  - Cross-modal projectors connect the two modalities:\n    - “Q-former Projector”\n    - “Cross-Attn Projection”\n  - Intermediate feature boxes are labeled “Cell Features” and “Text Features.”\n  - Arrows indicate cell-to-text and text-to-cell projection paths.\n  - A central label reads “Cross-modal Projectors.”\n\n- Right panel: output tasks/findings\n  - “1. Cell-Text Discrimination”: produces a “Relevance Score.”\n  - “2. Text-to-Cell Generation”: decodes text-derived information into a gene-expression/cell representation.\n  - “3. Cell-To-Text Generation”: decodes cell representation into a textual description, shown as the classical monocyte example.\n\nOverall, the figure depicts a multimodal architecture aligning scRNA-seq gene-token representations with textual biological descriptions using an scLLM, a text LLM, and cross-modal projection modules for discrimination and bidirectional generation tasks."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 5, "caption": "Figure 4: Summary of data used in the pre-training of scMMGPT. The dataset includes 27 million single-cell transcriptomic profiles from diverse human organs and tissues.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a biomedical infographic showing a human body diagram with organ and tissue callouts. It appears to summarize cell or tissue categories across anatomical locations, with counts in millions.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Central full-body anatomical illustration with highlighted organs, including brain, lungs, heart, liver, intestines, kidneys, reproductive region, and other tissue markers.\n- Green connector lines link organs/body regions to labeled text boxes around the figure.\n- Left-side labels:\n  - Peripheral Retina 1.20M\n  - Heart 0.68M\n  - Liver 0.44M\n  - Blood 4.18M\n  - Kidney 0.35M\n- Right-side labels:\n  - Brain 7.48M\n  - Lung 1.20M\n  - Breast 2.25M\n  - Others 6.46M\n  - Pan-Cancer 2.68M\n- Each label includes a small organ/cell icon and short biological text describing example cell types, such as amacrine cells, pericytes, hepatocytes, B cells, podocytes, neurons, pulmonary alveolus, myoepithelial cells, basal cells, and plasma cells.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Human organs and tissues: retina, heart, liver, blood, kidney, brain, lung, breast, miscellaneous tissues, and pan-cancer samples.\n- Cell types are mentioned in the explanatory snippets.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- No visible computational pipeline, model architecture, embeddings, plots, or transformation arrows are shown.\n- The image is a schematic/summary figure rather than a quantitative chart.\n\nFindings shown:\n- The figure visually associates organ/tissue categories with apparent dataset/sample sizes, e.g. Brain 7.48M, Others 6.46M, Blood 4.18M, Pan-Cancer 2.68M.\n- It provides brief descriptive definitions or examples for cell types in each tissue category."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Figure 5: The two-stage cross-modal pre-training scheme. (a) In cross-modal discriminative pretraining, the model achieves cell-text integration by distinguishing matched cell-text pairs from unrelated pairs through contrastive and matching objectives. (b) In cross-modal generative pretraining, the model continues knowledge integration via unified generative tasks, including cell-to-text and text-to-cell generation objectives.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic scientific figure with two labeled panels describing a cross-modal pretraining framework for single-cell and text models.\n\nPanel (a), “Stage 1: Cross-modal Discriminative Pre-training,” shows scRNA-seq input for “a B cell” represented as gene tokens/expression bars feeding into an scLLM. Text descriptions are divided into negative descriptions, such as mismatched cell types including megakaryocyte and natural killer cell, and a positive description identifying the cell as a B cell. The scLLM output and text descriptions connect through a Q-former projector with self-attention, cross-attention, and FFN blocks. The objective is labeled “Cell-Text Matching” and “Cell-Text Contrasting.”\n\nPanel (b), “Stage 2: Cross-modal Generative Pre-training,” has two workflows. The top “Cell-to-Text Generation” path sends scRNA/gene information through an scLLM, then a Q-former projector, then “Cell Features” into a Text LLM to generate a textual cell description. The bottom “Text-to-Cell Generation” path sends a B-cell text description through a Text LLM to produce embeddings, then through a cross-attention projector into an scLLM interface with gene tokens and expression levels.\n\nBiological source objects include scRNA-seq measurements from a B cell, gene tokens, expression-level bars, and cell-type descriptions. The figure illustrates transformations between single-cell gene-expression representations and natural-language descriptions using scLLM, Text LLM, Q-former, and cross-attention projection modules."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 21, "caption": "Figure 6: UMAP visualization of scMMGPT's embeddings for cells from different experimental batches on PBMC-10K [56]. The result demonstrates the model's ability to capture cell type distinctions while effectively mitigating batch effects.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows two side-by-side UMAP scatter plot panels.\n\nLeft panel:\n- Title: “Cell Type”\n- Axes: “Umap 1” and “Umap 2”\n- Points are colored by annotated immune cell type.\n- Legend includes: B Cells, Cd4 T Cells, Cd8 T Cells, Cd14+ Monocytes, Dendritic Cells, Fcgr3a+ Monocytes, Megakaryocytes, Nk Cells.\n- Visible clusters include a large lower-right cluster, a lower-left cluster, an upper-left cluster, and smaller intermediate/right-side clusters.\n\nRight panel:\n- Title: “Batch”\n- Axes: “Umap 1” and “Umap 2”\n- Same UMAP embedding layout as the left panel.\n- Points are colored by batch labels “0” and “1”.\n- Orange and blue points appear mixed across most clusters, suggesting batch distribution is broadly overlapping rather than forming completely separate clusters.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell observations, likely immune cells, represented as points in a low-dimensional embedding.\n- Cell-type annotations correspond to immune cell populations.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Dimensionality reduction/embedding using UMAP.\n- Coloring by metadata: cell type in the left panel and batch in the right panel.\n\nVisible finding:\n- Cell types occupy distinct regions of the UMAP embedding.\n- Batch labels are interspersed across the embedding, with no obvious complete separation by batch."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 21, "caption": "Figure 7: UMAP plot of embeddings from scMMGPT for PBMC-10K [56] dataset in zero-shot task. Plots are colored by actual cell type labels and predicted cell type labels.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows a two-panel scientific visualization comparing cell-type annotations on a 2D UMAP embedding.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Left panel titled “Truth”\n- Right panel titled “Predicted”\n\nAxes and labels:\n- Both panels have x-axis labeled “Umap 1”\n- Both panels have y-axis labeled “Umap 2”\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell observations represented as colored points\n- Cell-type categories shown in the legend:\n  - B cells\n  - CD4 T cells\n  - CD8 T cells\n  - CD14+ Monocytes\n  - Dendritic Cells\n  - FCGR3A+ Monocytes\n  - Megakaryocytes\n  - NK cells\n\nTransformation:\n- Cells are projected into a 2D UMAP coordinate space.\n- The same embedding layout appears to be shown for ground-truth and predicted labels.\n\nModel interface:\n- The figure compares true cell-type labels against predicted cell-type labels, likely from a cell classification or annotation model.\n\nFindings visible:\n- Predicted labels closely match the truth panel visually.\n- Major clusters retain similar color/category assignments between truth and prediction.\n- Large clusters include B cells at the top, CD4/CD8 T-cell-associated clusters at the lower right, and CD14+ monocytes at the lower left."]}, {"figure_index": 12, "page_no": 21, "caption": "Figure 8: UMAP plot of embeddings from LangCell [12] for PBMC-10K [56] dataset in zero-shot task. Plots are colored by actual cell type labels and predicted cell type labels.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows a two-panel UMAP visualization comparing cell-type annotations.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Left panel titled “Truth”\n- Right panel titled “Predicted”\n\nAxes and labels:\n- Both panels have x-axis labeled “Umap 1”\n- Both panels have y-axis labeled “Umap 2”\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell observations represented as points in UMAP space\n- Cell types shown in the legend:\n  - B cells\n  - CD4 T cells\n  - CD8 T cells\n  - CD14+ Monocytes\n  - Dendritic Cells\n  - FCGR3A+ Monocytes\n  - Megakaryocytes\n  - NK cells\n\nTransformations / model interface:\n- The figure compares ground-truth cell labels against predicted cell labels on the same low-dimensional UMAP embedding.\n- The right panel appears to show model-predicted cell-type classifications corresponding to the truth labels in the left panel.\n\nFindings visible:\n- Most major clusters appear similarly labeled between truth and prediction.\n- A noticeable discrepancy appears in the left-side orange CD4 T cell region, where the predicted panel includes green CD8 T cell labels along part of that cluster.\n- Other clusters, including B cells, CD14+ monocytes, FCGR3A+ monocytes, megakaryocytes, and NK cells, appear broadly consistent between panels."]}, {"figure_index": 13, "page_no": 22, "caption": "Figure 9: Heatmap of the results from scMMGPT's Cell Type Annotation on Myeloid [52], hPancreas [53], Multiple Sclerosis [54] and PBMC-3k [55] dataset.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows a 2x2 grid of confusion-matrix-style heatmaps with color bars from 0.0 to 1.0. Each panel has axes labeled “Truth label” on the y-axis and “Predicted label” on the x-axis.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Top left: “Myeloid”\n- Top right: “hPancreas”\n- Bottom left: “Multiple Sclerosis”\n- Bottom right: “PBMC-3K”\n\nThe matrices show strong dark-blue diagonal entries indicating high agreement between true and predicted labels for many classes. Off-diagonal light-blue entries indicate some misclassification or weaker cross-label prediction signals, most noticeably in the Myeloid and Multiple Sclerosis panels. The hPancreas and PBMC-3K panels appear mostly diagonal with few off-diagonal signals.\n\nBiological source objects referenced by panel titles include myeloid cells, human pancreas, multiple sclerosis-related samples, and PBMC-3K peripheral blood mononuclear cells. The figure appears to evaluate a classification model or label-transfer/prediction interface across biological datasets using normalized confusion matrices."]}, {"figure_index": 14, "page_no": 23, "caption": "Figure 10: Visualization of a single-cell RNA sequencing matrix. Rows represent individual cells, and columns represent genes. The color intensity corresponds to the log1p-transformed expression levels, with darker shades indicating higher expression.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a single-panel heatmap-style scientific figure.\n\nVisible content:\n- A matrix heatmap with rows labeled as “Single Cell Instances” on the y-axis.\n- The x-axis is labeled “Genes”.\n- Gene labels are shown along the bottom, rotated vertically. Visible labels include examples such as `MALAT1`, `MT-CO2`, `MT-CO1`, `MT-ND3`, `MT-ND4L`, `MT-CO3`, `MT-CYB`, `MT-ATP8`, `MT-ND4`, `MT-ND6`, `MT-ND5`, `MT-ND2`, `MT-ND1`, `RNR2`, `SP90AA1`, `HSPA1A`, `HSPA1B`, `DNAJB1`, `FOSB`, `SP90AB1`, `HSPA8`, `JUNB`, `FOS`, `DUSP1`, `BTG1`, `JUN`, `FTL`, `B2M`, `RPL41`, and `RPLP1`.\n- A colorbar on the right ranges from approximately `0.0` to `3.0`, using dark purple for low values and yellow/green for high values.\n- The heatmap appears to show expression or normalized intensity values across single-cell instances and genes.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell instances.\n- Genes, including mitochondrial genes, heat-shock genes, immediate early genes, and ribosomal/housekeeping-associated genes.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to represent transformed quantitative gene-level values as a color-coded matrix.\n- No explicit model architecture, computational workflow, or interface is visible in the image.\n\nVisible finding:\n- Different single-cell instances show heterogeneous gene-value patterns.\n- Some rows have stronger signal for genes near the left side, including mitochondrial genes.\n- Several central/right gene groups show low-value purple blocks in subsets of cells, indicating reduced or absent signal for those genes in those instances."]}]

